WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.540
 

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.630
Will we run out of fossil fuels?

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:07.270
Or more precisely, will a gradual scarcity of fossil fuels

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:11.560
drive up their price so that alternatives out-compete them?

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:15.900
Much of our modern thinking about this is driven by a very famous 1956 lecture

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.210
by a petroleum geologist called M. King Hubbert.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.330
He used a bell shaped curve to predict the US oil production.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.020
And correctly predicted the peak in 1970.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:31.300
In 1973, the Arab oil shock drove fears of oil scarcity

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.010
that persist to this day.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.020
But Hubbert did not predict correctly the future of US oil production, which

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.050
has since rebounded.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.490
But since that time, fears about peak oil,

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.470
fears about an end to the oil age driven by scarcity,

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:49.020
have centered around Hubbert&amp;#39;s prediction of a peak.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.930
My view is that we are threatened more by the abundance of fossil fuels

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:54.190
than by scarcity.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.960
Of course there will be local scarcity of some fuels.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.670
And there&amp;#39;s a turbulence in the push and pull between oil supply and oil demand.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.300
But there are at least four forces that in the long run

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.150
suggest that fossil fuels will be widely available.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.360
The first is technological change.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.720
The second is the geological diversity of the resource.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.640
The third is price elasticity.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.840
And the fourth is interfuel substitution and conversion.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.940
In the next sections, we&amp;#39;ll explore these four factors in order

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.000
and look at how they explain the history of oil production in the world.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.140
First, technological change-- the technologies for finding and extracting

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.020
oil and gas just keep getting better.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.890
Two examples, one is the shale gas revolution,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.350
where technologies for drilling long horizontal wells

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.600
and for making fractures in precise locations

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.600
that allow the oil or gas to seep out of rock that otherwise would be too

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.980
tight, too impermeable, to allow it to flow naturally

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.860
have allowed an enormous expansion of US oil and gas

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.230
production using new technologies.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:02.100
Second, the gradual increase in the size and efficiency, and now automation,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.710
of mining equipment allows very large scale coal production

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:07.720
at ever declining costs.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:12.180
Second, geological diversity-- when one hears about an oil resource,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.490
it&amp;#39;s very tempting to imagine a fixed reservoir, like a tank,

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.240
and imagine that as that tank is gradually depleted,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.330
you get to some point where it&amp;#39;s all gone.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.550
That&amp;#39;s true for some conventional so-called volumetric oil reservoirs.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:29.180
But hydrocarbons occur in a vast diversity of forms.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.220
There are immense reservoirs, for example, of Canadian oil or tar

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.100
sands that are well known deposits.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.960
It&amp;#39;s easy to know precisely where they are.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.950
But they&amp;#39;re very expensive to extract and turn into oil.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.930
There are other deposits, like ultra deep finds recently made

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:47.820
off the coast of Brazil, where production is well understood, but finding them

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.710
is very difficult. When you look across the whole world,

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.730
there is enormous diversity of different kinds of oil and gas reservoirs.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.160
And that diversity means that there&amp;#39;s no reason

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.570
to think about a single fixed supply.

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:03.200
There are larger and larger supplies of materials

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.960
that are harder and harder to get at.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.690
Third, price elasticity-- when prices go up, people work harder to get the oil.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.750
You can see this in this time series that shows the oil price,

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.590
along with a number of working US oil rigs.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.220
See how the two move together.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.290
They are correlated.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.840
Indeed, the technical definition of oil reserves

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.120
is the amount of oil that can be produced using current technology

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.170
at the current market price.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.030
This means that if oil price suddenly goes up,

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.840
oil reserves actually go up overnight, with no change in technology

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.970
and no change in the actual oil in the ground.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.570
Oil reserves are, in this sense, partly an economic definition.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.010
The McKelvey box provides a good way to think

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:49.700
about the role of price, technology, and geology in determining the availability

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.130
of a resource like oil and gas.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.070
This is a system that is used by economic geologists studying

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.440
both oil and gas and other resources like, say minerals.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.010
The vertical axis shows the cost of producing

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.910
a resource with current technology.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:06.490
While the horizontal axis shows the certainty that resource exists,

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.210
geological certainty.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:11.660
So you can think about existing oil and gas reserves or resources

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.330
on these two axes.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.930
The Canadian oil or tar sands, for example,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.560
are geologically certain in that we know precisely where they are.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.450
But the cost of accessing them, the cost of extracting that oil from the sands

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:26.760
and turning it into a marketable product, has been very high.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.030
Although it&amp;#39;s gradually declining as the technology improves.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.620
Finally, the fourth factor driving the availability of fossil fuels

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.320
is interfuel substitution and conversion.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.500
Substitution is when a user, for example a electricity plant,

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.660
can choose between using gas or coal and oil.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:47.350
And so they shift to the cheapest fuel.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.370
This means that when one fuel is scarce, they can shift to another fuel.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.180
And this provides a kind of economic interchange between the three fuels.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.150
As well as an economic interchange when users actually switch,

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.260
it&amp;#39;s physically possible to turn one fuel into another.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.740
For example, we can turn coal into a gaseous fuel, so-called coal

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:05.670
gasification.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:10.250
And we can turn gaseous fuels into liquid fuels, so called gas to liquids.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:13.460
So in the world today, we both turn coal all the way

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.890
into liquid fuels, much higher value fuels that are used for transportation.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:20.250
And we turn natural gas into liquid fuels.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.070
This conversion allows another kind of substitution separate

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.710
from the economic substitution.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.640
It means that even if one fossil fuel runs short,

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.600
for example that we run out of oil.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.390
Running out of oil doesn&amp;#39;t mean running out

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:37.380
of hydrocarbon fuels produced from fossil fuels.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.156
Because we could produce that same oil from coal or gas.

00:05:41.156 --> 00:05:43.960
To review, we&amp;#39;ve examined four factors that

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.000
shape the long-run availability of fossil fuels.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.050
First, technological change; second, geological diversity;

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:55.820
third, price elasticity; and fourth, interfuel substitution.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.600
Together these have shaped the history of the oil age since the 1850s.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.450
As an example, look at the global history of oil production.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:10.490
Note, for example, that between about 1930 and 1973 the price of oil

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.610
was roughly constant in inflation-adjusted terms.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.200
While the quantity of oil increased 10 fold.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:18.800
How did that happen?

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.920
It&amp;#39;s the story we&amp;#39;ve been telling, a story

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:24.250
of technological change, increasing ease of access,

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.440
competing against scarcity of the easy resources.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.090
And over that time period, the competition between those two

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.740
came out about even.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:38.190
But note, the sharp discontinuities, discontinuities in 1973 and since then,

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:41.950
and some discontinuities in the late 19th century.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:46.510
Those were caused by regional pushes and pull, a turbulence between the dynamics

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.840
of oil production and oil demand.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.170
There&amp;#39;s no doubt that the turbulence in oil prices will continue.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:56.640
I don&amp;#39;t claim to be able to predict oil prices a year or five years hence.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.100
A single disaster, natural disaster or attack on, for example,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:05.170
the major Saudi loading terminal, could drive oil prices enormously high.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:08.580
While a global economic recession could drive them much lower.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.730
What these factors do is predict that the long run

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.730
availability of fossil fuels is large.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:18.600
And that it&amp;#39;s unlikely that, in the long run, sheer scarcity of fossil fuels

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.870
will limit our ability to use them, at least not for centuries.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:29.280
 

